{"questions":[{"_i":130,"q":"Which blood value is the best measure of long-term glucose regulation in diabetes?","a":["Fasting glucose","HbA1c","Postprandial glucose","Fructosamine"],"ex":"HbA1c: glycated hemoglobin reflects average glucose concentration over the past 2-3 months (erythrocyte lifespan). Target value in DM: <53 mmol/mol (7%). Fructosamine reflects 2-3 weeks (useful in hemoglobinopathies or pregnancy).","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":131,"q":"Troponin can also be elevated in renal failure without myocardial infarction.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Elevated troponin in renal insufficiency: reduced clearance + chronic myocardial stress. But: with an acute rise (>20% over 3-6 hours), AMI must be actively excluded. Context is everything: renal function + clinical picture + ECG.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":132,"q":"30-year-old man: years of alcohol abuse. Blood count: MCV 110 fL, Hb low, normoblastic bone marrow. Diagnosis?","a":["Iron deficiency anemia","Megaloblastic anemia due to folate/B12 deficiency","Hemolytic anemia","Aplastic anemia"],"ex":"Megaloblastic anemia: macrocytes (MCV >100) due to deficiency of B12 or folate → impaired DNA synthesis. Alcohol: malnutrition → folate deficiency. B12 deficiency: atrophic gastritis, vegans, malabsorption. Treatment: folate/B12 supplementation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":133,"q":"LP result:\nClear CSF\nLymphocytosis 80 cells\nProtein 0.7 g/L (slightly elevated)\nGlucose normal\nMost likely?","a":["Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis","SAH","TB meningitis"],"ex":"Viral (aseptic) meningitis: clear/opalescent fluid, lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal glucose, slightly elevated protein. Pathogens: enterovirus, HSV, EBV. Usually self-limiting. HSV encephalitis: CSF + temporal lobe abnormality on MRI → acyclovir immediately.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":134,"q":"TSH is the most sensitive test for detecting thyroid disease, including subclinical cases.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. TSH has a logarithmic relationship with free T4 → small changes in T4 → large TSH change. Subclinical hypothyroidism: TSH elevated, free T4 normal. TSH is the first-line test; free T4 and T3 for confirmation/typing.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":135,"q":"Which combination fits intravascular hemolysis?","a":["Low LDH, high haptoglobin","High LDH, low haptoglobin, high indirect bilirubin","High ferritin, low transferrin","Low reticulocyte count"],"ex":"Intravascular hemolysis: red blood cells destroyed in the bloodstream → Hb released → LDH high (from erythrocytes), haptoglobin falls (binds free Hb), indirect bilirubin rises (breakdown product). Reticulocytes increased (compensatory production). Hemoglobinuria: brown urine.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":136,"q":"62-year-old woman: alkaline phosphatase 3× elevated, GGT 4× elevated, ALT normal. Ultrasound: gallstones, no bile duct dilatation. Diagnosis?","a":["Hepatitis","Cholestatic pattern, probably primary biliary cholangitis","Muscle disease","Alcoholic hepatitis"],"ex":"Cholestatic pattern: ALP + GGT elevated (biliary cause), transaminases relatively normal. In woman >40 years + positive AMA (anti-mitochondrial antibodies) = primary biliary cholangitis. Treatment: ursodeoxycholic acid. Fibrosis and cirrhosis possible.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":137,"q":"An international normalized ratio (INR) of 3.5 in a patient on warfarin without bleeding always requires immediate hospital admission.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Asymptomatic elevated INR: depends on level and context. INR 3.5-4.9 without bleeding: temporarily stop warfarin, recheck. With INR >5 or bleeding symptoms: administer vitamin K. Acute: prothrombin complex concentrate. Admission with active bleeding or very high INR.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":138,"q":"55-year-old man: creatinine rises from 90 to 280 μmol/L within 48 hours after contrast CT. Urine: granular casts. Diagnosis?","a":["Prerenal renal failure","Contrast-induced nephropathy (acute tubular necrosis)","Postrenal renal failure","Glomerulonephritis"],"ex":"Contrast nephropathy: acute tubular cell necrosis due to osmotic stress + vasoconstriction. Granular casts = tubular sloughing. Prevention: IV hydration before+after, minimum contrast volume, stop metformin. Recovery usually occurs within 1-2 weeks.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":139,"q":"25-year-old woman: chronic fatigue, weight loss, hyperpigmentation, low blood pressure. Na 128, K 5.6, fasting glucose 3.2. Diagnosis?","a":["Hyperthyroidism","Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease)","SIADH","Anorexia nervosa"],"ex":"Addison's disease: destruction of adrenal cortex → cortisol and aldosterone deficiency. Triad: hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypoglycemia. Hyperpigmentation: high ACTH stimulates melanocytes. Addisonian crisis: hemodynamically unstable → immediate IV hydrocortisone + IV NaCl.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":140,"q":"An elevated PSA (prostate-specific antigen) proves prostate cancer.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. PSA is organ-specific, not cancer-specific. Elevated in: prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia, prostatitis, catheterization. PSA is a screening tool — diagnosis requires tissue biopsy. PSA ratio (free/total) improves specificity.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":141,"q":"38-year-old man: joint pain, urate 560 μmol/L, kidney stones. What is the first treatment step to lower urate?","a":["NSAID for the pain","Allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibitor)","Probenecid","Colchicine"],"ex":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase → less urate production. First choice for gout prophylaxis and urate nephropathy. Start only after the acute attack has settled (otherwise risk of triggering an attack). Febuxostat as alternative. Hydrate well, limit red meat and alcohol.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":142,"q":"Blood gas:\npH 7.28\nPaCO₂ 32 mmHg\nHCO₃⁻ 15 mmol/L\nAnion gap 22\nWhich disorder?","a":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis with elevated anion gap","Respiratory alkalosis"],"ex":"Metabolic acidosis + high anion gap (normal 8-12): AG = Na - (Cl + HCO3) = 22 → elevated. Causes MUDPILES: Methanol, Uremia, DKA, Propylene glycol, INH/Iron, Lactate, Ethanol/Ethylene glycol, Salicylates. Low PaCO2 = compensatory respiratory alkalosis.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":143,"q":"65-year-old man: eGFR 48 ml/min/1.73m², stable for 4 months. Diagnosis?","a":["Acute renal failure","Chronic kidney disease (CKD) stage G3a","Nephrotic syndrome","Prerenal renal failure"],"ex":"CKD: eGFR <60 ml/min for >3 months = chronic kidney disease. G3a = eGFR 45-59. Causes: DM, hypertension, glomerulonephritis. Complications: anemia (EPO deficiency), hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis. No curative treatment, but progression can be slowed.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":144,"q":"Blood results:\nALT 580 U/L\nAST 320 U/L\nBilirubin slightly elevated\nAlkaline phosphatase normal\nMost likely pattern?","a":["Cholestatic pattern (bile duct obstruction)","Hepatocellular pattern (hepatitis)","Muscle disease","Hemolytic anemia"],"ex":"Hepatocellular pattern: ALT and AST markedly elevated, ALP relatively normal. ALT is more liver-specific than AST. Causes: viral hepatitis, alcoholic hepatitis, autoimmune hepatitis, drug-induced injury. AST/ALT ratio >2 → think of alcoholic hepatitis.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":145,"q":"In severe hyponatremia, sodium should be corrected as quickly as possible to prevent brain damage.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Too rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia (>8-10 mmol/L per day) can cause osmotic demyelination (central pontine myelinolysis) — irreversible brain damage. Exception: acute symptomatic hyponatremia (seizures, coma) → cautious hypertonic NaCl 3%.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":146,"q":"Patient after chemotherapy: fever, neutrophil granulocytes 0.3 × 10⁹/L. What is this condition called and what do you do?","a":["Leukopenia — wait and see","Febrile neutropenia — immediate broad-spectrum antibiotics","Agranulocytosis — give G-CSF","Lymphopenia — antiviral therapy"],"ex":"Febrile neutropenia: fever + absolute neutrophils <0.5 × 10⁹/L in a chemotherapy patient = medical emergency. Obtain blood cultures immediately and then start broad-spectrum antibiotics (piperacillin/tazobactam). Do not wait hours for culture results — mortality rises quickly.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":147,"q":"Nephrotic syndrome:\nProteinuria 4.5 g/day\nAlbumin 21 g/L\nEdema, hypercholesterolemia\nWhat is NOT a feature?","a":["Heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/day)","Hypoalbuminemia","Hypertension and hematuria","Hypercholesterolemia"],"ex":"Nephrotic syndrome: large protein loss through kidney → hypoalbuminemia → edema + hypercholesterolemia (liver compensates). Hematuria and hypertension are features of nephritic syndrome (glomerular inflammation). Distinction is clinically important for further diagnostics.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":148,"q":"Blood gas:\npH 7.26\npCO₂ 68 mmHg\nHCO₃⁻ 29 mmol/L\nInterpretation?","a":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","Mixed disorder"],"ex":"Respiratory acidosis: low pH + high pCO2 = hypoventilation. HCO3 elevated = renal compensation (chronic). Causes: COPD exacerbation, opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease, obesity hypoventilation. Treatment: underlying cause + possibly NIV ventilation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":149,"q":"Procalcitonin (PCT) is a more specific marker for bacterial infection than CRP.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. CRP rises with any form of inflammation (bacterial, viral, autoimmune, trauma). PCT rises specifically in systemic bacterial infections and sepsis. PCT is also used to stop antibiotics earlier: falling PCT = good response. Normal PCT <0.25 µg/L.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":150,"q":"55-year-old woman with liver cirrhosis: PT 22 seconds (prolonged), bleeding symptoms. Why is the PT prolonged?","a":["Vitamin K deficiency due to malabsorption","The liver produces insufficient coagulation factors","Thrombocytopenia due to hypersplenism","Heparin effect"],"ex":"The liver produces almost all coagulation factors (I, II, V, VII, VIII, IX, X, XI). In cirrhosis: reduced synthesis → prolonged PT/INR. Factor VII has the shortest half-life → falls first. INR in liver patients reflects the degree of liver function loss.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":151,"q":"70-year-old man with lung cancer:\nCalcium 3.1 mmol/L\nPTH low\nPhosphate normal\nMost likely cause?","a":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Vitamin D intoxication","Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia (PTHrP)","Sarcoidosis"],"ex":"Hypercalcemia of malignancy: tumor produces PTH-related peptide (PTHrP) that activates PTH receptors → calcium from bones + reduced renal clearance. PTH itself is low (negative feedback). Treatment: IV fluids + bisphosphonates, treat the malignancy.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":152,"q":"An elevated CK (creatine kinase) is specific for cardiac injury.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. CK is present in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle and brain. CK rises with any muscle disease: rhabdomyolysis, myositis, trauma, intense exercise, myocardial infarction. For cardiac injury, troponin I/T is much more specific. CK-MB is slightly more specific for heart but still less than troponin.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":153,"q":"22-year-old woman: painless vaginal discharge, pain on urination. Culture: gram-negative diplococcus. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Chlamydia","Gonorrhea","Trichomonas","Bacterial vaginosis"],"ex":"Gonorrhea: gram-negative diplococcus, often asymptomatic in women. Treatment: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once — NOT amoxicillin (>40% resistant in NL). Always co-test for chlamydia (co-infection 20-40%). Partner notification legally mandatory.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":154,"q":"Chlamydia trachomatis infection is asymptomatic in most women.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Chlamydia is the most common sexually transmitted infection in the Netherlands. Up to 70% of women have no symptoms. Untreated risk of PID (pelvic inflammatory disease), tubal damage and reduced fertility. Treatment: azithromycin 1 g once or doxycycline 7 days.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":155,"q":"28-year-old woman: fever 39°C, right flank pain, pain on urination, nausea. Urine culture: E. coli. Diagnosis?","a":["Uncomplicated cystitis","Pyelonephritis","Appendicitis","Kidney stone"],"ex":"Pyelonephritis = upper UTI. Distinguish from cystitis: fever ≥38°C + flank pain (costovertebral angle tenderness) points to renal parenchyma. Cystitis: only dysuria/pollakiuria, no fever. Treatment: fluoroquinolone 7 days; IV antibiotics if vomiting or sepsis.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":156,"q":"Norovirus (stomach flu) spreads mainly through the air (airborne transmission).","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Norovirus spreads primarily fecal-orally: contaminated food/water or surfaces. Also via vomit particles (aerosol particles can be released during vomiting). Alcohol hand sanitizer is NOT effective — soap and water required. Infectious at as few as 18 viral particles.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":157,"q":"Patient returns from Southeast Asia: high fever, severe headache/muscle pain, rash, platelets 68 × 10⁹/L. No malaria on blood smear. Diagnosis?","a":["Malaria","Dengue","Typhoid","Leptospirosis"],"ex":"Dengue: flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquito. Features: high fever, severe muscle/joint pain ("breakbone fever"), maculopapular rash, low platelets. No specific treatment — symptomatic. No aspirin or ibuprofen (bleeding risk with low platelets).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Dengue is a flavivirus infection transmitted by the Aedes aegypti mosquito (day-active, also in urban areas). Four serotypes (DENV 1-4) — infection with one type after recovery increases the risk of severe dengue with infection by another serotype (antibody enhancement).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Dengue fever progresses in phases: (1) febrile phase (2-7 d): high fever, headache, muscle pain; (2) critical phase (day 4-6): fever falls but vascular leakage."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"thrombocytopenia + hemoconcentration."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"risk of dengue shock; (3) recovery phase: resorption + polyuria. No malaria parasite on blood film."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Malaria","desc":"also tropical + fever, but cyclic pattern, parasite on blood film, no typical rash."},{"label":"Chikungunya","desc":"also Aedes transmission + joint pain, but chronic joint symptoms months after infection."},{"label":"Zika","desc":"mild fever + rash + conjunctivitis, dangerous in pregnancy (microcephaly)."},{"label":"Typhoid","desc":"food/water contamination, abdominal pain, bradycardia."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No antiviral therapy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Symptomatic","detail":"paracetamol (NOT aspirin or ibuprofen — worsens bleeding risk!)."},{"naam":"Monitor fluid balance — in dengue shock","detail":"cautious IV fluids."},{"naam":"Platelet monitoring","detail":"<20×10⁹/L or active bleeding → consider transfusion."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Notifiable disease in NL."}]},{"_i":158,"q":"Immunocompromised patient (after kidney transplant): fever, white patches in the mouth that scrape off. Diagnosis?","a":["Herpes simplex","Oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush)","Aphthous stomatitis","Streptococcal pharyngitis"],"ex":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis: Candida albicans overgrowth with immunosuppression, antibiotic use, corticosteroids or dry mouth. White coating that comes off with rubbing, painful. Treatment: oral fluconazole or local nystatin suspension.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":159,"q":"Hepatitis B can be transmitted through contaminated blood transfusions, sexual contact and from mother to child during delivery.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. HBV is 50-100× more infectious than HIV via blood. Transmission routes: blood (needlestick injury, transfusion), sexual, vertical (mother→child). Prevention: vaccination + HBIG in newborns of HBsAg-positive mothers. HBV vaccine is effective and part of the national immunization program.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":160,"q":"Patient after dental procedure: fever, new heart murmur, positive blood cultures with Streptococcus viridans. Diagnosis?","a":["Rheumatic fever","Infective endocarditis","Myocarditis","Pericarditis"],"ex":"Infective endocarditis (IE): vegetations on heart valves. Duke criteria: positive blood cultures + echocardiographic evidence of vegetation. Streptococcus viridans: classic after dental procedure. Treatment: prolonged IV antibiotics (4-6 weeks). Caveat emboli.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":161,"q":"In a tetanus vaccine-naive patient with a deep wound, only the vaccine should be given.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. In an unvaccinated patient with tetanus risk: give both tetanus toxoid vaccine (active immunization) AND tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG, passive immunization). The vaccine builds protection for the future; TIG provides immediate protection now.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":162,"q":"Varicella (chickenpox) in a 35-year-old adult: which serious complication must be actively excluded?","a":["Otitis media","Varicella pneumonia","Meningitis","Glomerulonephritis"],"ex":"Varicella pneumonia: much more severe in adults and immunocompromised patients than in children. Presents with cough, dyspnea and fever during or shortly after the rash. Treatment: IV acyclovir. Pregnant women especially vulnerable → immediate treatment.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Varicella (chickenpox, VZV) usually has a mild course in children, but in adults pneumonia is the most dangerous complication (1-5% of adult chickenpox). In pregnant women and immunocompromised patients, the risk is even higher.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"VZV spreads hematogenously to the lungs → viral pneumonia + interstitial inflammation."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Presents day 3-5 after onset of the rash: dyspnea, dry cough, fever, sometimes hemoptysis."},{"title":"Chest X-ray","desc":"bilateral nodular infiltrates."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"Distinguishing from bacterial superinfection (S. aureus, S. pyogenes) can sometimes be difficult."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bacterial pneumonia","desc":"purulent sputum, lobar infiltrate, high CRP/leukocytes."},{"label":"Mycoplasma","desc":"atypical pneumonia, dry cough, no rash."},{"label":"Influenza pneumonia","desc":"influenza season, negative VZV history."},{"label":"Herpes zoster reactivation","desc":"dermatomal, no diffuse rash, older individual."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acyclovir IV 10 mg/kg 3×/day for 7-10 days in varicella pneumonia or severe disease.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Oral acyclovir (800 mg 5×/day) in mild adult varicella without pneumonia."},{"naam":"Pregnant women","detail":"always treat + alert neonatologist."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"VZV prophylaxis (VZIg) available for seronegative people after exposure."}]},{"_i":163,"q":"NSAIDs (such as ibuprofen) can raise blood pressure and reduce the effect of antihypertensive medications.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin production → fluid retention + vasoconstriction → blood pressure increase. This weakens the effect of ACE inhibitors, diuretics and beta-blockers. Use also associated with worsening renal function, gastrointestinal bleeding and cardiovascular risk.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":164,"q":"Patient uses long-term prednisolone (corticosteroid). Which complication should you NOT expect?","a":["Osteoporosis","Hyperglycemia","Hypotension","Increased infection risk"],"ex":"Long-term corticosteroids actually cause hypertension (not hypotension) due to sodium and water retention. Other adverse effects: osteoporosis, DM, weight gain, Cushingoid appearance, cataracts, gastric ulcer and immunosuppression.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":165,"q":"Patient uses lithium for bipolar disorder. Which two organs must be monitored regularly?","a":["Heart and liver","Kidneys and thyroid","Lungs and adrenal glands","Liver and pancreas"],"ex":"Lithium is nephrotoxic with chronic use (tubular damage) and can cause hypothyroidism. Check creatinine and TSH every 6 months. Lithium level itself also needs monitoring: narrow therapeutic window (0.6-1.0 mmol/L). Cave dehydration — rapidly increases lithium level.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":166,"q":"Older man on digoxin: nausea, yellow-green color vision, bradycardia 44/min. Diagnosis?","a":["Digoxin toxicity","Beta-blocker overdose","Hyperkalemia","Hypothyroidism"],"ex":"Digoxin toxicity: narrow therapeutic window. Symptoms: nausea, yellow/green vision, bradycardia, AV blocks. Risk factors: renal failure (reduced clearance), hypokalemia (sensitizes heart), old age. Treatment: stop digoxin, digoxin Fab fragments in severe toxicity.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"Digoxin has a therapeutic range of 0.5–0.9 ng/mL (narrow!). Toxicity occurs at serum levels >2.0 ng/mL.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition","desc":"Digoxin blocks the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump → increased intracellular Na⁺ in cardiac cells."},{"title":"Ca²⁺ accumulation","desc":"High intracellular Na⁺ → accelerated Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger → increased intracellular Ca²⁺ → positive inotropic effect."},{"title":"Toxicity threshold","desc":"At toxic levels (>2.0 ng/mL): excessive Ca²⁺ → afterdepolarizations → triggered activity → ventricular arrhythmias."},{"title":"Risk factors","desc":"Renal failure (reduced clearance), hypokalemia (sensitizes heart to arrhythmias), older adults. Narrow therapeutic window: 0.5–0.9 ng/mL."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Digoxin toxicity","desc":"Bradycardia + AV block + xanthopsia (yellow/green color vision) + GI symptoms. Narrow therapeutic window. Risk with renal failure + hypokalemia."},{"label":"Beta-blocker toxicity","desc":"Bradycardia + AV block but no xanthopsia or prominent GI symptoms. Glucagon as antidote."},{"label":"Calcium channel blocker overdose","desc":"Bradycardia + hypotension + hyperglycemia. IV calcium + insulin as antidote."},{"label":"Hyperkalemia","desc":"Wide QRS + sine-wave ECG — can also cause ECG changes, but no xanthopsia."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop digoxin immediately.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Electrolyte correction","detail":"Correct hypokalemia — aim for K⁺ >4.0 mmol/L to raise the arrhythmia threshold."},{"naam":"Bradycardia","detail":"Atropine IV for symptomatic bradycardia — temporary measure."},{"naam":"Digibind/DigiFab","detail":"Digoxin-specific Fab antibody fragments in life-threatening arrhythmia or severe hyperkalemia — rapidly neutralize circulating digoxin."}]},{"_i":167,"q":"Patients with a penicillin allergy must never receive cephalosporins because of a high chance of cross-reaction.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. The cross-reactivity rate between penicillins and cephalosporins is only 1-2%, much lower than previously thought. With a mild penicillin reaction (rash, no anaphylaxis), cephalosporins can be used safely. Extra caution is needed only with anaphylaxis.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":168,"q":"Why are benzodiazepines discouraged as sleeping medication in older adults over 65 years?","a":["They do not work in older adults","Increased risk of falls, confusion and cognitive decline","They cause dependence only in young people","They are too expensive for older adults"],"ex":"Benzodiazepines in older adults: slower breakdown → accumulation → still sedating during the day → fall risk + hip fracture + confusion + cognitive decline. Alternative: sleep hygiene, melatonin or short-term low-dose Z-drug. Listed on the Beers list (avoid in older adults).","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":169,"q":"The combination of an SSRI and an NSAID increases the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. SSRIs inhibit platelet serotonin uptake → reduced platelet aggregation. NSAIDs damage the gastric wall and also inhibit platelets via COX-1. Combined use gives a 3-15× higher chance of GI bleeding. Consider gastric protection (PPI) with this combination.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":170,"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has known adverse effects on the lungs, thyroid and liver with long-term use?","a":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Amiodarone","Verapamil"],"ex":"Amiodarone: effective broad-spectrum antiarrhythmic but with many adverse effects with long-term use: pulmonary fibrosis, hypo- and hyperthyroidism (contains 37% iodine), liver toxicity, photosensitivity, blue-gray skin discoloration, corneal deposits. Regular monitoring of TSH, liver enzymes and lung function needed.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":171,"q":"Which diuretic spares potassium and is also used in heart failure to improve prognosis?","a":["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone","Mannitol"],"ex":"Spironolactone (aldosterone antagonist): blocks aldosterone → potassium-sparing + sodium excretion. In heart failure (HFrEF): reduces fibrosis and mortality. Caveat: hyperkalemia when combined with ACE inhibitor/ARB. Adverse effect in men: gynecomastia (aldosterone receptor also has androgen affinity).","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":172,"q":"Paracetamol is safe in any dose and has no liver-damaging effect.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Paracetamol is safe within the recommended dose (max 4 g/day, max 3 g/day in liver disease/alcohol abuse). In overdose: toxic metabolite NAPQI accumulation → liver necrosis. Antidote: N-acetylcysteine (NAC). Paracetamol overdose is one of the most common causes of acute liver failure.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":173,"q":"45-year-old man after car accident: back pain, weakness in both legs, loss of bladder control. Urgent diagnosis?","a":["Lumbar disc herniation","Cauda equina syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy","Vertebral metastasis"],"ex":"Cauda equina syndrome: ALARM SYMPTOM is urinary retention (or incontinence) + saddle anesthesia. Leg weakness is variable. Neurosurgical emergency: MRI within hours, decompression within 24 h — any delay = permanent loss of bladder function.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Cauda equina syndrome (CES) is compression of the nerve bundle below L1 (sacral roots = peripheral nerves). Classic triad: urinary retention + perianal sensory disturbance (saddle anesthesia) + leg weakness.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cause & anatomy","desc":"Most common cause: large central lumbar disc herniation (L4-L5 or L5-S1). Also: trauma, epidural abscess, tumor, hematoma. Pressure on S2-S4 roots."},{"title":"Bladder innervation","desc":"Compression of S2-S4 → loss of parasympathetic bladder innervation → urinary retention (or incontinence with overflow bladder)."},{"title":"Diagnostics","desc":"Lumbar MRI is the only reliable gold standard — perform within hours. Saddle anesthesia (perianal area) is pathognomonic."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Cauda equina syndrome","desc":"Urinary retention + saddle anesthesia + leg weakness. Peripheral lesion (LMN). Immediate lumbar MRI. Decompression within 24 h."},{"label":"Conus medullaris syndrome","desc":"Higher level (T12-L1), mixed UMN/LMN deficits, less retention, earlier bilateral weakness."},{"label":"Lumbar disc herniation without CES","desc":"Unilateral radiating pain, no bladder dysfunction — elective management."},{"label":"Vertebral metastasis","desc":"Cancer history, bone pain, myelopathic signs — also an oncologic emergency."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Emergency admission + immediate lumbar MRI — hours count!","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Decompression","detail":"Surgical decompression within 24 h in ambulatory patient → 80% chance of preserving walking ability."},{"naam":"Catheterization","detail":"Urethral catheter as bridge for urinary retention."},{"naam":"After decompression","detail":"Bladder/bowel rehabilitation, neurophysiological follow-up and multidisciplinary rehabilitation."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"Delay in decompression = permanent bladder loss — every delay counts!"}]},{"_i":174,"q":"68-year-old woman: spinning dizziness lasting seconds, triggered by turning over in bed, no hearing loss. Diagnosis?","a":["Cerebellar stroke","Ménière's disease","BPPV (benign paroxysmal positional vertigo)","Vestibular neuritis"],"ex":"BPPV: brief vertigo triggered by head movements, caused by a dislodged otolith in a semicircular canal. Diagnosis: Dix-Hallpike maneuver. Treatment: Epley maneuver. No medication needed.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"BPPV is the most common cause of vertigo. A dislodged otolith (calcium carbonate crystal) in the posterior semicircular canal disrupts fluid movement with head movements → brief intense spinning dizziness.","wiki_redflag":"Dizziness not triggered by position change, or accompanied by headache, double vision, ataxia or focal deficit → think of cerebellar stroke. Use HINTS test.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Canalithiasis","desc":"Dislodged otolith sits in the posterior semicircular canal → when turning over it slides through → abnormal endolymph movement → vertigo for seconds."},{"title":"Dix-Hallpike maneuver","desc":"Turn head 45° + suddenly lay back → provokes torsional nystagmus + dizziness in BPPV. Nystagmus occurs after 5-10 sec latency."},{"title":"Epley maneuver","desc":"Series of head movements that "roll" the otolith out of the canal → 90% success after 1-2 treatments. No medication needed."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"BPPV","desc":"Seconds-lasting dizziness, triggered by position, no hearing loss. Dix-Hallpike positive. Epley curative."},{"label":"Ménière's disease","desc":"Attacks 20 min - hours, hearing loss + tinnitus + ear fullness. Longer than BPPV."},{"label":"Vestibular neuritis","desc":"Persistent (days) continuous dizziness, no hearing loss. After viral infection. Not position-dependent."},{"label":"Cerebellar stroke","desc":"HINTS test: nystagmus changes direction or no head-impulse correction → alarming! Immediate CT/MRI."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Perform Epley maneuver immediately — 90% cure after 1-2 sessions.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Dix-Hallpike first","detail":"Confirm the diagnosis: torsional nystagmus toward the affected ear, latency 5-10 sec, lasts <60 sec, fatigability."},{"naam":"Epley maneuver","detail":"4-step repositioning maneuver, hold each for 30 sec. Then sleep upright for 48 h (theoretical but evidence limited)."},{"naam":"No medication","detail":"Meclizine/betahistine have no proven benefit over Epley in BPPV. Do not use routinely."}]},{"_i":175,"q":"An epileptic seizure lasting longer than 5 minutes is considered status epilepticus and requires immediate treatment.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Status epilepticus: seizure >5 minutes or two seizures without recovery of consciousness in between. Treatment: lorazepam or diazepam IV/rectal immediately. If no response: levetiracetam or valproate IV. After 30 min without response: anesthesia.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Status epilepticus (SE) = seizure ≥5 minutes or two seizures without recovery of consciousness. Every additional minute = more neuronal damage. Strict timeline: benzodiazepine immediately, then IV antiepileptic drug, then anesthesia.","wiki_redflag":"Refractory SE (>30 min without response) requires anesthesia induction and mechanical ventilation. Mortality 10-20% — delay in treatment increases risk of permanent brain damage.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Self-perpetuating mechanism","desc":"Prolonged seizure → internalization of GABA receptors → reduced inhibitory activity → benzodiazepines become less effective the longer the seizure lasts."},{"title":"Neuronal damage","desc":"Excessive glutamate activation → calcium influx → excitotoxicity → irreversible neuronal death with persistent SE."},{"title":"Timeline","desc":"0-5 min: normal seizure. 5-30 min: early SE → treat immediately. 30-60 min: established SE. >60 min: refractory SE."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Convulsive SE","desc":"Clinically visible: ongoing tonic-clonic movements. Treat immediately. Risk of rhabdomyolysis, hyperthermia, hypoxia."},{"label":"Non-convulsive SE","desc":"No external muscle movements but continuous EEG activity. Confusion/coma. Diagnosis only with EEG. Just as serious!"},{"label":"Psychogenic seizure","desc":"No EEG correlate. Non-epileptic seizure. Does not respond to antiepileptic drugs. Diagnosis with video-EEG."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"0-5 min: benzodiazepine — lorazepam 4 mg IV or diazepam 10 mg rectal.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"5-30 min (no response)","detail":"Levetiracetam 60 mg/kg IV OR valproate 40 mg/kg IV OR phenytoin 20 mg/kg IV. Choose levetiracetam as first choice (fewest adverse effects)."},{"naam":">30 min (refractory)","detail":"Anesthesia induction: propofol or midazolam IV, intubation, continuous EEG monitoring in ICU."},{"naam":"Look for cause","detail":"Blood glucose (hypoglycemia), sodium (hyponatremia), fever (meningitis), antiepileptic drug levels, toxicology."}]},{"_i":176,"q":"40-year-old man: progressive choreiform (writhing) movements, behavioral change, family member also affected. Diagnosis?","a":["Parkinson's disease","Huntington's disease","Wilson's disease","Tardive dyskinesia"],"ex":"Huntington's disease: autosomal dominant disorder due to CAG repeat in the HTT gene. Triad: chorea + behavioral change + dementia. Typically begins at 35-50 years. Genetic test confirms diagnosis. No curative treatment.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder due to a CAG trinucleotide repeat (>36 copies) in the HTT gene on chromosome 4. Anticipation: larger repeat in next generation → earlier onset.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Mutant huntingtin","desc":"CAG repeat >36 copies → mutant huntingtin protein is toxic to the striatum (caudate nucleus + putamen)."},{"title":"GABA neuron loss","desc":"Loss of GABAergic neurons in the striatum → reduced inhibition of thalamus → uncontrolled movements."},{"title":"Frontal atrophy","desc":"Behavioral change and executive dysfunction often occur BEFORE motor symptoms due to frontal lobe atrophy."},{"title":"Anticipation","desc":"With paternal transmission: CAG repeat expands → more severe disease and earlier onset in next generation."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Huntington's disease","desc":"Autosomal dominant, CAG repeat >36 on chromosome 4. Triad: chorea + behavioral change + dementia. Anticipation. Positive family history."},{"label":"Sydenham chorea","desc":"Post-streptococcal (children), temporary course, anti-ASO elevated, no family history."},{"label":"Wilson's disease","desc":"Also chorea but combined with liver cirrhosis + Kayser-Fleischer ring + impaired copper metabolism."},{"label":"Tardive dyskinesia","desc":"After antipsychotics, orolingual dominant, no family history — history is crucial."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No curative treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Chorea","detail":"tetrabenazine or deutetrabenazine (presynaptic dopamine depletion)."},{"naam":"Psychiatric comorbidity","detail":"antidepressants, low-dose antipsychotics."},{"naam":"Multidisciplinary","detail":"physiotherapy, speech therapy, psychological care."},{"naam":"Genetic counseling","detail":"Essential — children have 50% risk; presymptomatic testing possible after thorough consultation and ethical consideration."}]},{"_i":177,"q":"32-year-old woman: fever, confusion, temporal lobe abnormalities on MRI, abnormal CSF. Most likely pathogen?","a":["Neisseria meningitidis","Herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1)","Cryptococcus","Listeria"],"ex":"HSV encephalitis: temporal localization on MRI is characteristic. Presents with fever, confusion and sometimes epilepsy. Treatment: start IV acyclovir as soon as possible — even before confirmation by CSF PCR.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"HSV encephalitis is the most common sporadic viral encephalitis. Cause: HSV-1 reactivation in trigeminal ganglion → retrograde spread to temporal lobes.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Viral reactivation","desc":"HSV-1 reactivates in the trigeminal ganglion and spreads retrogradely to the temporal lobe — primary locus of damage."},{"title":"Necrotizing inflammation","desc":"Hemorrhagic, necrotizing encephalitis in the temporal lobe and insular cortex → edema, epilepsy and herniation risk."},{"title":"Diagnostics","desc":"LP: lymphocytic pleocytosis, slightly elevated protein, normal glucose. HSV-DNA PCR is the gold standard. MRI: T2-FLAIR hyperintensity temporal lobe (sometimes bilateral)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"HSV encephalitis","desc":"Fever + confusion + temporal MRI abnormalities. LP: lymphocytic pleocytosis, slightly elevated protein, HSV-DNA PCR positive. Acyclovir immediately!"},{"label":"Bacterial meningitis","desc":"PMN pleocytosis, markedly low glucose, no temporal MRI abnormalities — antibiotics urgent."},{"label":"Autoimmune encephalitis (anti-NMDAR)","desc":"Slow onset over weeks, younger women, ovarian teratoma, psychiatric symptoms predominate (hallucinations, behavioral change). HSV PCR negative."},{"label":"Cryptococcal meningitis","desc":"Immunocompromised (HIV, immunosuppression), India ink LP positive, low glucose."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acyclovir IV 10 mg/kg 3×/day — immediately when suspected, do NOT wait for PCR.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Treatment duration","detail":"14 days in normal immunity, 21 days in immunosuppression. PCR after treatment to confirm endpoint."},{"naam":"Antiepileptic drugs","detail":"For seizures — levetiracetam or valproate, temporal origin gives high recurrence risk."},{"naam":"Residual symptoms","detail":"Memory problems, behavioral change, epilepsy — multidisciplinary rehabilitation after acute phase."}]},{"_i":178,"q":"35-year-old man: attacks of throbbing unilateral headache with nausea, photophobia, lasting 4-72 hours. Diagnosis?","a":["Tension headache","Cluster headache","Migraine","Sinusitis"],"ex":"Migraine: unilateral, throbbing headache 4-72 h + nausea/vomiting + photophobia and phonophobia. Aura in 30%. Acute treatment: triptan + NSAID. Prophylaxis with ≥4 attacks/month: propranolol, topiramate or amitriptyline.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Migraine is a neurobiological disorder characterized by recurrent attacks of unilateral, throbbing headache (4-72 h) with nausea and photo-/phonophobia. Aura (visual/sensory) in 30% of patients.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cortical spreading depression","desc":"Wave of neuronal depolarization spreads across cortex → aura. Activates trigeminal nerve → pain mediators released → vasodilation → throbbing headache."},{"title":"Trigeminovascular pathway","desc":"Trigeminal nerve endings around dura mater and cerebral arteries → neuropeptides (CGRP) released → neurogenic inflammation → throbbing pain."},{"title":"Prodrome","desc":"Hours before the attack: yawning, fatigue, loss of concentration, irritability — neuronal hyperexcitability in hypothalamus."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Migraine","desc":"Unilateral throbbing, 4-72 h, nausea, photo/phonophobia. Worsens with movement. Triptan effective."},{"label":"Tension headache","desc":"Bilateral pressing (band around head), mild-moderate intensity, no nausea, no phono/photophobia."},{"label":"Cluster headache","desc":"Extremely severe, periorbital, 15-180 min, autonomic symptoms (tearing, ptosis). Male, nocturnal."},{"label":"SAH","desc":"Thunderclap (maximal in seconds), neck stiffness. Never dismiss as migraine with first attack."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Triptan (sumatriptan 50-100 mg) + NSAID (naproxen 500 mg) together — more effective than separately.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Attack treatment","detail":"Triptan effective in 60-70%. Domperidone/metoclopramide for nausea. Take early in the attack."},{"naam":"Prophylaxis","detail":"With ≥4 attacks/month: propranolol 80-160 mg/day, topiramate 25-100 mg/day, or amitriptyline 10-75 mg/night."},{"naam":"CGRP antibodies","detail":"Erenumab, fremanezumab — new, effective in refractory migraine. Monthly subcutaneous injection."}]},{"_i":179,"q":"A subdural hematoma is caused by rupture of the middle meningeal artery and presents with a lucid interval.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. That is an epidural hematoma (arterial). A subdural hematoma is caused by rupture of bridging veins (venous) and has a slower course — sometimes only days to weeks after trauma. More common in older adults and people using anticoagulants.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Subdural hematoma (SDH) is caused by rupture of bridging veins (venous) — not the middle meningeal artery (that is epidural). SDH has a slower course: acute (<24 h), subacute (1-14 days) or chronic (>14 days).","wiki_redflag":"Chronic SDH in older adults: can present as dementia or progressive confusion weeks after minor trauma. Anticoagulants and brain atrophy greatly increase risk.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Bridging vein rupture","desc":"With (minor) trauma or acceleration-deceleration: bridging veins running over the dura are stretched and tear → venous bleeding in subdural space."},{"title":"Risk groups","desc":"Older adults (brain atrophy → more space → more tension on bridging veins), anticoagulation, alcoholism, shunts."},{"title":"Chronic SDH","desc":"Old blood dissolves, osmotically attracts fluid → hematoma grows → increased ICP with delayed symptoms weeks later."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Subdural hematoma","desc":"Crescent-shaped on CT (follows brain contour), venous, older adults/anticoagulation, no or slow lucid interval."},{"label":"Epidural hematoma","desc":"Biconvex on CT, arterial (middle meningeal artery), temporal skull fracture, young people, classic lucid interval."},{"label":"SAH","desc":"Blood in sulci and cisternae, no trauma required, thunderclap headache, xanthochromia in CSF."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute SDH with neurological deficit: neurosurgical evacuation.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stop anticoagulation","detail":"Stop and reverse immediately: vitamin K + PCC for VKA. Andexanet for factor Xa inhibitors."},{"naam":"Chronic SDH","detail":"Burr-hole trepanation (burr-hole drainage) under local anesthesia — less invasive than craniotomy, effective in chronic cases."},{"naam":"Conservative","detail":"Small asymptomatic SDH in stable patient: CT follow-up, stop anticoagulation, observation."}]},{"_i":180,"q":"60-year-old man with prostate cancer: acute back pain, leg weakness, impaired sensation below the knees. MRI: mass at T8. Diagnosis?","a":["Lumbar disc herniation","Metastatic spinal cord compression","Aortic dissection","Peripheral neuropathy"],"ex":"Metastatic spinal cord compression: HOURS count — with new loss of walking ability, risk of permanent injury. Immediate dexamethasone 16 mg IV (anti-edema). Emergency radiotherapy or surgical decompression. Able to walk at treatment → high chance of preserving walking; already paraplegic → poor prognosis.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Metastatic epidural spinal cord compression (MESCC) is an oncologic emergency. Most common primary tumors: breast, prostate, lung, myeloma, renal cell.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hematogenous metastasis","desc":"Tumor cells reach vertebral bodies via the bloodstream — breast, prostate and lung are the most common primary tumors."},{"title":"Epidural extension","desc":"Tumor extends from vertebral body into the epidural space and directly compresses the spinal cord."},{"title":"Vascular damage","desc":"Venous congestion and arterial ischemia of the spinal cord → edema and axonal damage."},{"title":"Time-critical injury","desc":"The longer the compression lasts, the more axons are irreversibly damaged — walking function at onset is the critical prognostic measure."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Metastatic spinal cord compression (MESCC)","desc":"Cancer history + acute myelopathy. MRI: epidural mass. Dexamethasone + emergency radiotherapy or surgery."},{"label":"Lumbar disc herniation","desc":"Younger, no malignancy, unilateral radicular, no myelopathic signs."},{"label":"Cauda equina syndrome","desc":"Lower level, LMN deficit, bladder retention early — also an emergency."},{"label":"Epidural hematoma","desc":"Acute, bleeding diathesis, no tumor history — CT/MRI distinguishes quickly."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate dexamethasone 16 mg IV bolus — anti-edema, works within hours.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Radiotherapy","detail":"Emergency radiotherapy within 24 h for non-surgical tumors — effective in most solid metastases."},{"naam":"Surgical indications","detail":"Mechanical instability, unknown primary tumor, radioresistant tumor or progression after radiotherapy."},{"naam":"Prognosis ambulatory","detail":"Ambulatory at start of treatment → 80% preserve walking function."},{"naam":"Prognosis paraplegia","detail":"Already paraplegic at onset → only 15% chance of recovery."}]},{"_i":181,"q":"55-year-old woman with DM2: burning sensation and tingling in both feet, symmetric, worse at night. Diagnosis?","a":["Peripheral arterial disease","Distal symmetric polyneuropathy","Spinal cord compression","Restless legs syndrome"],"ex":"Diabetic polyneuropathy: most common complication of DM, begins distally symmetric (stocking/glove pattern). Cause: chronic high glucose damages small blood vessels of nerves. Treatment: glucose control, amitriptyline or duloxetine for pain.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common chronic complication of DM — present in 50% after 25 years. Stocking/glove pattern: begins in the feet, ascends.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Chronic hyperglycemia","desc":"Long-term elevated glucose activates harmful metabolic pathways: aldose reductase (sorbitol), AGE formation and oxidative stress."},{"title":"Vasa nervorum damage","desc":"Microangiopathy of the small blood vessels that supply nerves → ischemia and axonal degeneration, starting in the longest fibers (feet)."},{"title":"Autonomic neuropathy","desc":"Autonomic fibers also affected: gastroparesis, orthostatic hypotension, sexual dysfunction, silent MI (no pain perception!)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Diabetic polyneuropathy (DSPN)","desc":"Stocking/glove pattern, burning at night, symmetric distal, slowly progressive with DM duration >10 years."},{"label":"PAD","desc":"Pain when walking (claudication), disappears at rest, ABI <0.9. No burning character, not worse at night."},{"label":"B12 deficiency","desc":"Identical picture of neuropathy — ALWAYS exclude, fully reversible with early treatment. Metformin reduces B12 absorption."},{"label":"Restless legs","desc":"Unpleasant feeling in evening/night with urge to move, improves with movement — no true neuropathy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Strict glucose regulation slows progression — HbA1c <53 mmol/mol as target.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Pain treatment","detail":"Duloxetine (SNRI) or pregabalin/gabapentin as first choice; amitriptyline as alternative."},{"naam":"Capsaicin","detail":"Topical capsaicin for local pain — depleted TRPV1 fibers produce fewer pain signals."},{"naam":"Fall prevention","detail":"Balance training + strength training — neuropathy significantly increases fall risk."},{"naam":"Foot check","detail":"Annual monofilament test + inspection — neuropathic ulcer is a precursor to amputation."}]},{"_i":182,"q":"Cluster headache is more common in women than in men.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Cluster headache is actually one of the few headache types more common in men (M:F ≈ 3:1). Presentation: extremely severe unilateral pain around the eye, with tearing and rhinorrhea, conjunctival injection, duration 15-180 minutes.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Cluster headache is the most severe primary headache — the pain around the eye is described as a "hot poker in the eye". Characteristic: ipsilateral autonomic symptoms (tearing, ptosis, rhinorrhea) + periods of daily attacks (clusters).","wiki_redflag":"The intensity of cluster headache can lead to suicidality ("suicide headache"). Patients are typically agitated and cannot lie still — unlike migraine.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hypothalamic activation","desc":"Hypothalamus activates trigeminal pain pathways and parasympathetic nerves → periorbital pain + autonomic symptoms."},{"title":"Autonomic symptoms","desc":"Parasympathetic activation: tearing, rhinorrhea, conjunctival injection. Sympathetic inhibition: ptosis, miosis (Horner)."},{"title":"Cluster period","desc":"Attacks daily 1-8x per day for weeks to months, followed by months of remission (episodic type)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Cluster headache","desc":"Periorbital, extremely severe, 15-180 min, ipsilateral autonomic symptoms, male, nocturnal, agitation."},{"label":"Migraine","desc":"Longer (4-72 h), throbbing, nausea, patient lies down quietly, more common in women."},{"label":"Trigeminal neuralgia","desc":"Electric shock-like facial pain, seconds, triggered by eating/talking/touching. No autonomic symptoms."},{"label":"SAH","desc":"Thunderclap, neck stiffness. Never diagnose cluster headache without excluding SAH in first attack."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Oxygen 100% 15 L/min via non-rebreather mask for 15 min — effective in 60-70% of attacks.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Sumatriptan SC","detail":"6 mg subcutaneously — fastest and most effective attack treatment. Works within 15 minutes."},{"naam":"Prophylaxis during cluster","detail":"Verapamil 240-960 mg/day — most effective. Lithium in chronic type. Corticosteroids for rapid bridging."},{"naam":"Prevention","detail":"Avoid alcohol and nicotine during cluster period — proven triggers."}]},{"_i":183,"q":"42-year-old woman: sudden shortness of breath, pleuritic pain, swollen leg after long flight. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Pneumonia","Pulmonary embolism","Pleuritis","Pneumothorax"],"ex":"Pulmonary embolism: sudden pleuritic pain + dyspnea + risk factor (flight, DVT) = high clinical suspicion. Low Wells score + negative D-dimer: PE excluded. High Wells or positive D-dimer: immediate CT-PA. Treatment: DOAC (apixaban or rivaroxaban).","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Pulmonary embolism (PE): occlusion of a pulmonary artery by a (usually venous) thrombus. Risk factors: immobilization, surgery, malignancy, pregnancy, previous DVT. Diagnosis via Wells score + D-dimer or CT pulmonary angiography.","wiki_redflag":"Massive PE: hemodynamic instability (BP <90 mmHg or drop >40 mmHg) = life-threatening. Immediate thrombolysis or surgical embolectomy — anticoagulation alone insufficient.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"DVT → PE","desc":"Deep venous thrombus (usually iliofemoral) detaches → flows via right heart to pulmonary arteries → obstruction of pulmonary circulation."},{"title":"RV failure","desc":"Sudden increased pulmonary resistance → RV pressure overload → RV dilation → septal shift → LV filling falls → cardiac output falls → hemodynamic collapse in massive PE."},{"title":"Pleuritic pain","desc":"Peripheral PE occlusion → pleural infarction → pleuritic pain (sharp, worsens with inspiration). Central PE: more dyspnea and hemodynamic instability, less pleuritic pain."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Sudden dyspnea + pleuritic pain + tachycardia + risk factor (immobilization, DVT, surgery). D-dimer + CT-PA or V/Q scan."},{"label":"Pneumonia","desc":"Fever, productive cough, infiltrate on chest X-ray, high CRP, gradual onset — no typical thrombosis risk factors."},{"label":"Pneumothorax","desc":"Sudden dyspnea + unilateral decreased breath sounds, tympanic percussion note, no leg edema. Chest X-ray shows air band without lung markings."},{"label":"Pleuritis","desc":"Pleuritic pain with pleural friction rub, usually after viral infection — no DVT sign."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Oxygen + venous access + start anticoagulation immediately (LMWH or UFH).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"DOAC first choice","detail":"Rivaroxaban 15 mg bid (21 days) then 20 mg od, or apixaban 10 mg bid (7 days) then 5 mg bid."},{"naam":"Duration of anticoagulation","detail":"Provoked (temporary risk factor, e.g. flight): 3 months. Idiopathic or recurrent: 6 months to lifelong."},{"naam":"Massive PE","detail":"Thrombolysis (alteplase 100 mg/2 h) in hemodynamic instability — exclude absolute bleeding contraindications."}]},{"_i":184,"q":"A normal D-dimer in a patient with high clinical probability of pulmonary embolism rules out pulmonary embolism.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. D-dimer is only useful with low clinical probability (Wells ≤4). With high clinical probability, always CT angiography regardless of D-dimer. A negative D-dimer with high probability can be false-negative.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"D-dimer is an exclusion test, not a confirmation test. Useful only with low pre-test probability (Wells ≤4). Sensitivity >99% but specificity only ~40% — with high clinical probability, most positive D-dimers are truly pulmonary emboli, and a negative D-dimer is not reliable enough.","wiki_redflag":"Error: "D-dimer is normal so no PE." With high clinical probability, a normal D-dimer can be false-negative — always CT-PA with high clinical probability, regardless of D-dimer.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"D-dimer: what it is","desc":"Breakdown product of cross-linked fibrin. Elevated with active thrombosis but also with infection, trauma, pregnancy, postoperative state, malignancy, older age → low specificity."},{"title":"Sensitivity vs specificity","desc":"Sens >99%: virtually all PE patients have elevated D-dimer. Spec ~40%: many false positives, so only useful for exclusion in low risk (high NPV, low PPV)."},{"title":"Bayesian reasoning","desc":"With high pre-test probability, the post-test probability of PE after a negative D-dimer is still too high to exclude PE — direct imaging is the only safe option."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"D-dimer with high clinical probability: NOT useful","desc":"Wells >4 or high clinical suspicion → direct CT-PA, D-dimer testing adds nothing and can mislead."},{"label":"D-dimer with low clinical probability (Wells ≤4)","desc":"Negative D-dimer (<500 µg/L ELISA) + low clinical probability = PE safely excluded. No imaging needed."},{"label":"Age-adjusted cutoff","desc":"YEARS algorithm: in patients >50 years, higher cutoff (age × 10 µg/L) → fewer unnecessary CT scans."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"With high clinical probability: start anticoagulation while CT-PA is requested.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"CT-PA","detail":"Gold standard for PE diagnosis — sensitivity 95-98% for subsegmental and larger emboli."},{"naam":"V/Q scan","detail":"Alternative in contrast allergy or renal failure — normal V/Q scan excludes PE."}]},{"_i":185,"q":"Patient after major surgery: blood pressure 75/50, heart rate 120, cold extremities, oliguria. Diagnosis?","a":["Septic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock"],"ex":"Cardiogenic shock: low cardiac output → cold, clammy extremities + oliguria + low BP. Distinguish from septic shock: septic has warm extremities (vasodilation), cardiogenic has cold (vasoconstriction). Treatment: dobutamine (inotropic) + norepinephrine, consider IABP/Impella.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Cardiogenic shock: inadequate cardiac output due to primary heart failure (STEMI, acute MR, myocarditis) → low peripheral perfusion + pulmonary edema. Key feature: cold, clammy extremities (vasoconstriction) + elevated JVP/pulmonary edema — this distinguishes it from septic shock.","wiki_redflag":"Cardiogenic shock mortality 40-60% without treatment. Recognition = chance of survival: STEMI + cardiogenic shock → immediate PCI. Mechanical complications (VSD, papillary rupture) → emergency surgery.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Primary pump failure","desc":"LV contractility falls (infarction, myocarditis, decompensated heart failure) → low stroke volume → cardiac output falls → reduced systemic perfusion."},{"title":"Compensatory vasoconstriction","desc":"Sympathetic activation → peripheral vasoconstriction → cold, clammy extremities. In contrast to septic shock (vasodilation → warm extremities)."},{"title":"Pulmonary edema","desc":"Low cardiac output → backup of blood in pulmonary circulation → elevated pulmonary wedge pressure → pulmonary edema. Combination of low output + pulmonary edema = cardiogenic shock."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Cardiogenic shock","desc":"Cold, clammy extremities + pulmonary edema + crackles + elevated JVP. Low cardiac output. Troponin high with STEMI cause."},{"label":"Septic shock","desc":"Warm, red extremities (distributive shock), fever, infection source, low SVR. High cardiac output early in sepsis."},{"label":"Hypovolemic shock","desc":"Cold extremities but low JVP (not elevated), no pulmonary edema — blood loss/dehydration as cause."},{"label":"Obstructive shock (tamponade/massive PE)","desc":"Similar to cardiogenic but cause mechanical — pericardiocentesis/thrombolysis, not inotropes."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Oxygen + IV access + treat cause (PCI in STEMI).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Norepinephrine","detail":"First-choice vasopressor in cardiogenic shock — target MAP ≥65 mmHg."},{"naam":"Dobutamine","detail":"Add inotrope with persistent low cardiac output despite vasopressor."},{"naam":"Mechanical circulatory support","detail":"IABP (intra-aortic balloon pump) or Impella in refractory cardiogenic shock as bridge-to-recovery or bridge-to-transplant."}]},{"_i":186,"q":"65-year-old man after inferior MI: holosystolic murmur at the apex, radiating to the axilla, new onset. Diagnosis?","a":["Aortic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation due to papillary muscle rupture","Ventricular septal rupture","Pericardial friction rub"],"ex":"Acute mitral regurgitation after MI: papillary muscle rupture → holosystolic murmur → acute pulmonary congestion. Medical emergency. Distinguish from VSD: in VSD, the murmur is also holosystolic but parasternal, with a thrill.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Acute mitral regurgitation due to papillary muscle rupture is a mechanical complication of MI (especially inferior MI = RCA occlusion supplies the posteromedial papillary muscle). Sudden regurgitation of blood into the LA → acute pulmonary congestion → pulmonary edema. Medical emergency: surgical valve replacement.","wiki_redflag":"New holosystolic murmur after MI = mechanical complication until proven otherwise. Echo is the key diagnostic test. Cardiac surgery the same day — mortality without surgery >70% within 24 h.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Papillary muscle rupture","desc":"Inferior MI → ischemia/necrosis of posteromedial papillary muscle (only one blood supply: RCA) → rupture (day 1-5) → mitral leaflet loses support → acute large-volume regurgitation into LA."},{"title":"Acute pulmonary edema","desc":"LA is not enlarged and not adapted to sudden volume overload → LA pressure rises quickly → backward failure → pulmonary edema. No compensatory dilation (unlike chronic MR)."},{"title":"Distinguishing VSD","desc":"VSD is also a mechanical complication after MI — murmur holosystolic but at the left sternal border (not apex) + thrill + left-to-right shunt (right catheter: step-up O₂ saturation in RV)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Acute MR (papillary rupture)","desc":"Holosystolic murmur apex, radiation to axilla, sudden pulmonary congestion after MI. Echo: flail mitral leaflet, eccentric regurgitant jet."},{"label":"Ventricular septal rupture (VSD)","desc":"Holosystolic murmur left parasternal + thrill, left-to-right shunt — PA catheter: O₂ step-up in RV. Usually anterior MI (LAD occlusion)."},{"label":"Pericardial friction rub","desc":"Scratchy murmur, triphasic (systolic + diastolic), audible in multiple positions — no regurgitant jet on echo."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IABP as bridge + urgent echocardiography + cardiac surgery consult.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Nitroprusside","detail":"Afterload reduction — lowers regurgitant fraction, improves forward output while emergency surgery is arranged."},{"naam":"Surgical valve replacement","detail":"Gold standard: valve replacement (usually mechanical valve) or valve repair. Emergency surgery mortality 20-30%, but survival without surgery nearly 0%."}]},{"_i":187,"q":"Nitrates (e.g. nitroglycerin) are absolutely contraindicated with sildenafil (Viagra) use.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Both agents dilate blood vessels via cGMP. Combination → severe, life-threatening blood pressure drop. Wait at least 24 hours after sildenafil (48 h after tadalafil) before nitrates. Always ask about this in a patient with chest pain.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Nitrates (NO donors) and PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil) both work through the cGMP system to relax smooth muscle → combined use causes a synergistic, severe blood pressure drop → cardiovascular collapse. Absolute contraindication.","wiki_redflag":"ALWAYS ask about PDE5 inhibitor use in chest pain. Sildenafil within 24 h before nitrates is a contraindication. Tadalafil (longer half-life): 48 h waiting period. Combination can be fatal.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Nitrates: NO → cGMP","desc":"Nitroglycerin is converted to NO → activates guanylate cyclase → cGMP rises → protein kinase G activates → myosin dephosphorylase → smooth muscle relaxation → vasodilation (mainly venous)."},{"title":"Sildenafil: PDE5 inhibition","desc":"PDE5 normally breaks down cGMP. Sildenafil inhibits PDE5 → cGMP accumulates → prolonged smooth muscle relaxation. Combined with nitrates: double cGMP increase → massive vasodilation → severe hypotension."},{"title":"Half-lives","desc":"Sildenafil: ~4 h (clinically maintain 24 h). Vardenafil: ~5 h (maintain 24 h). Tadalafil: ~18 h (maintain 48 h due to longer duration of action)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Nitrates + PDE5 inhibitor: absolute contraindication","desc":"Severe life-threatening hypotension. No exceptions. Waiting times: sildenafil 24 h, tadalafil 48 h after last dose."},{"label":"Nitrates + alcohol","desc":"Relative contraindication — also increased hypotension risk, but not absolute."},{"label":"Nitrates in RV infarction","desc":"Absolute contraindication — dependent on preload for cardiac output. Nitrates → preload reduction → severe hypotension in RV infarction."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Give nitroglycerin only if PDE5 use in the last 24-48 h has been excluded.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"History in ED","detail":"With chest pain, always ask about sildenafil/tadalafil. Do not give nitrates without certainty about PDE5 inhibitor use."},{"naam":"Nitrate indications","detail":"Angina pectoris (sublingual spray), acute coronary syndrome (IV), pulmonary edema/heart failure (reducing afterload/preload). Contraindications: PDE5 inhibitors, RV infarction, severe hypotension."}]},{"_i":188,"q":"58-year-old man after pulmonary embolism: treated with apixaban. After how many months may you stop in a first provoked embolism (e.g. after surgery)?","a":["1 month","3 months","6 months","Lifelong"],"ex":"First provoked pulmonary embolism (temporary risk factor such as surgery or cast): 3 months of anticoagulation is sufficient. In idiopathic embolism or recurrence: 6 months to lifelong depending on risk profile.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Duration of anticoagulation after pulmonary embolism depends on whether the PE was provoked or idiopathic. Provoked by a temporary factor (surgery, immobilization, cast): 3 months. Idiopathic/unprovoked or recurrent: extended treatment (6 months to lifelong).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Provoked PE","desc":"Temporary risk factor (surgery, long flight, cast, pregnancy) → risk disappears after elimination of the trigger → 3 months anticoagulation sufficient to bridge the early recurrence risk."},{"title":"Idiopathic PE","desc":"No clear temporary trigger → underlying hypercoagulability problem (thrombophilia, malignancy, antiphospholipid syndrome) → annual recurrence risk 5-10% → extended or lifelong anticoagulation."},{"title":"Malignancy-associated PE","desc":"Cancer increases PE risk ~4-7×. LMWH or DOAC (rivaroxaban, apixaban) — duration: as long as malignancy is active or being treated."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Provoked PE (first): 3 months","desc":"Temporary risk factor has disappeared (surgery healed, cast removed). Recurrence risk after stopping low. 3 months DOAC."},{"label":"Idiopathic/unprovoked PE: ≥6 months","desc":"No temporary trigger → increased recurrence risk → consider extended anticoagulation. Weigh bleeding risk vs thrombosis risk."},{"label":"Recurrent PE or DVT: lifelong","desc":"Recurrent thrombosis despite anticoagulation or after stopping → lifelong anticoagulation (DOAC)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"DOAC (apixaban or rivaroxaban) as first choice in hemodynamically stable PE.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Apixaban regimen","detail":"10 mg bid × 7 days, then 5 mg bid for the remaining period."},{"naam":"Stopping criteria","detail":"After 3 months in provoked PE: recovery complete + risk factor gone. Check for malignancy in unexplained recurrent PE."}]},{"_i":189,"q":"After myocardial infarction, a statin lowers the risk of a new cardiovascular event, even if LDL is already normal.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. In addition to LDL lowering, statins also have anti-inflammatory (pleiotropic) effects on the vascular wall. Guideline: every patient after MI receives a high-intensity statin, regardless of baseline LDL.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"After myocardial infarction, high-intensity statin therapy is recommended for everyone, regardless of baseline LDL. This is due to LDL lowering (target value <1.4 mmol/L) and pleiotropic effects: plaque stabilization, improved endothelial function, anti-inflammatory effect.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"mmol/L LDL target","wiki_bigfact_sub":"after myocardial infarction (very high cardiovascular risk) — ESC 2021","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"LDL lowering","desc":"Statin blocks HMG-CoA reductase → less intracellular cholesterol in liver → more LDL receptors → increased LDL uptake from blood → LDL reduction 30-55%."},{"title":"Pleiotropic effects","desc":"In addition to LDL lowering: stabilization of the atherosclerotic plaque (thicker fibrous cap, less lipid), anti-inflammatory effect (CRP ↓), improved endothelial function (NO production ↑)."},{"title":"Clinical evidence","desc":"HPS, 4S, LIPID studies: statins lower MACE (MI, stroke, cardiovascular death) after MI by 25-35%. This benefit exists regardless of baseline LDL."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Statin after MI: always, regardless of LDL","desc":"High-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40-80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) in everyone after MI. Target LDL <1.4 mmol/L and ≥50% reduction."},{"label":"Add ezetimibe"},{"label":"PCSK9 inhibitors","desc":"With LDL >1.4 mmol/L despite maximal statin + ezetimibe — alirocumab or evolocumab (50-60% additional LDL lowering)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start high-intensity statin during hospital admission after MI.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Atorvastatin 40-80 mg","detail":"Or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg — >50% LDL lowering."},{"naam":"Check LDL after 4-6 weeks","detail":"Target: LDL <1.4 mmol/L. Add ezetimibe 10 mg if target not reached."}]},{"_i":190,"q":"70-year-old woman: ECG shows a wide QRS tachycardia (130/min) without clear P waves. Most dangerous diagnosis to exclude first?","a":["AVNRT","Sinus tachycardia with bundle branch block","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter with aberrant conduction"],"ex":"Wide QRS tachycardia = ventricular tachycardia (VT) until proven otherwise. VT is life-threatening. Never just give adenosine in wide QRS — can worsen VT. If unstable: immediate cardioversion.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Wide QRS tachycardia (QRS >120 ms, rate >100/min) must always be treated as VT until proven otherwise. VT = arrhythmia originating from the ventricle itself, without the AV node as pacemaker.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Origin and triggers","desc":"VT arises from re-entry in scar tissue (most often in ischemic HFrEF), triggered activity or abnormal automaticity. Electrolyte disorders (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia), ischemia and medication (QT prolongation) are triggers."},{"title":"ECG criteria for VT","desc":"Brugada criteria or Vereckei criteria help distinguish VT from SVT with aberrancy: AV dissociation (P waves independent of QRS) is pathognomonic for VT. Fusion beats and capture beats confirm VT."},{"title":"Danger of adenosine in wide QRS","desc":"Adenosine can convert VT into VF — never give in wide QRS tachycardia without certainty about SVT. In WPW + AF, adenosine can also be life-threatening."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Ventricular tachycardia (VT)","desc":"Wide QRS >120 ms, rate 100-250/min, AV dissociation (pathognomonic), fusion beats, Brugada criteria positive. ALWAYS assume VT until proven otherwise."},{"label":"SVT with bundle branch block","desc":"Also wide QRS but P waves coupled to QRS, terminates with adenosine. Do NOT give adenosine if VT not excluded!"},{"label":"Pre-excitation (WPW)","desc":"Delta wave + short PR in sinus rhythm. AF in WPW can become extremely rapid — no adenosine or verapamil."},{"label":"Artifact","desc":"No hemodynamic consequences, chaotic baseline outside the "QRS" area, patient feels nothing."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Hemodynamically unstable: immediate synchronized DC cardioversion.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stable","detail":"amiodarone IV (300 mg bolus) as first choice."},{"naam":"Sotalol or procainamide","detail":"Alternative antiarrhythmics in VT — sotalol also as ICD prophylaxis in structural heart disease."},{"naam":"ICD implantation","detail":"After survived VT/VF (secondary prevention): always ICD. Primary prevention: ICD in HFrEF EF <35% after ≥3 months optimal medical therapy."},{"naam":"Treat underlying cause","detail":"ischemia, electrolyte disorders (K⁺/Mg²⁺), medication."}]},{"_i":191,"q":"50-year-old man: ECG shows ST elevation in II, III and aVF. Which coronary artery is probably occluded?","a":["Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left main coronary artery (LMCA)"],"ex":"Inferior MI (II, III, aVF) = RCA occlusion in 80% of cases. The RCA also supplies the right ventricle → always perform right-sided ECG leads (V3R/V4R) to rule out RV infarction.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"ECG localization of STEMI: the affected leads tell which coronary artery is occluded. Inferior (II, III, aVF) = RCA in 80% or LCx in 20%. Anterior (V1-V4) = LAD. Lateral (I, aVL, V5-V6) = LCx or diagonal branch.","wiki_redflag":"Inferior STEMI + right ECG (V4R): ST elevation ≥1 mm = RV infarction. Important: no nitrates or diuretics — RV is preload-dependent. Fluid resuscitation and dobutamine in RV shock.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"RCA supply","desc":"RCA supplies: inferior wall LV, inferior septum, RV free wall, SA node (55%), AV node (90%) → RCA occlusion can also cause rhythm disturbances (sinus bradycardia, AV block)."},{"title":"Reciprocal depression","desc":"ST elevation inferior (II/III/aVF) gives mirror-image ST depression in I and aVL — this confirms inferior localization and rules out pericarditis (diffuse elevation, no reciprocal depression)."},{"title":"LCx occlusion","desc":"Left circumflex artery supplies the posterolateral wall. Occlusion gives ST elevation in V5-V6 + I/aVL (lateral), sometimes also inferior. ST depression V1-V3 may be posterior MI (mirror image)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Inferior STEMI (RCA occlusion)","desc":"ST elevation in II, III, aVF. Reciprocal depression in I and aVL. V4R to rule out RV infarction. AV block possible (AV node RCA-supplied)."},{"label":"Anterior STEMI (LAD)","desc":"ST elevation V1-V4 — largest risk area (lateral wall + septum). Reciprocal depression in II/III/aVF. LV function severely affected."},{"label":"Lateral STEMI (LCx)","desc":"ST elevation I/aVL/V5-V6. ECG sometimes subtle — LCx occlusion is the most "missed" STEMI."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate PCI + right-sided ECG (V3R/V4R) in all inferior STEMIs.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"RV infarct protocol","detail":"No nitrates/diuretics. Fluid bolus (500 ml NaCl) with low blood pressure. Dobutamine with persistent hemodynamic instability."},{"naam":"AV block monitoring","detail":"Inferior MI: temporary AV block expected (reversible after PCI). Transcutaneous pacemaker standby with complete block."}]},{"_i":192,"q":"In pulmonary embolism with hemodynamic instability, systemic thrombolysis is the treatment of choice.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Massive pulmonary embolism with shock or cardiac arrest: thrombolysis (alteplase) or surgical embolectomy. In stable patients: anticoagulation (LMWH or DOAC). Thrombolysis in stable patients gives too much bleeding risk.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Massive pulmonary embolism (hemodynamic instability: systolic BP <90 mmHg or shock) requires reperfusion therapy: systemic thrombolysis (alteplase) or surgical embolectomy. Anticoagulation alone is insufficient in massive PE.","wiki_redflag":"Absolute contraindications to thrombolysis in PE: previous intracranial hemorrhage, recent brain surgery (<3 months), recent stroke (<3 months), active severe bleeding. In PE + cardiac arrest: give thrombolysis despite CPR (not a contraindication).","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Massive PE → RV failure","desc":"Large thrombus occludes central pulmonary artery → sudden RV afterload rise → RV dilation → septal shift to LV → LV output falls → cardiogenic shock → death without treatment."},{"title":"Thrombolysis mechanism","desc":"Alteplase (rt-PA) activates plasminogen → plasmin → fibrin degradation → rapid reduction in pulmonary resistance → RV pressure falls → hemodynamic recovery within minutes to hours."},{"title":"Submassive PE","desc":"Hemodynamically stable but RV dysfunction on echo or elevated BNP/troponin — role of thrombolysis is controversial (PEITHO study: less hemodynamic decompensation but more intracranial bleeding)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Massive PE: thrombolysis","desc":"Hemodynamic instability (BP <90 mmHg) or cardiac arrest. Alteplase 100 mg/2 h (or 0.6 mg/kg bolus during CPR). Immediately after diagnosis."},{"label":"Submassive PE (RV dysfunction, hemodynamically stable)","desc":"Anticoagulation + ICU monitoring. Consider thrombolysis only with clinical deterioration despite anticoagulation."},{"label":"Stable PE","desc":"DOAC (apixaban or rivaroxaban) as first choice — outpatient treatment possible in low-risk patients (HESTIA/PESI score)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours in massive PE — pause anticoagulation during and 24 h after thrombolysis.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Surgical embolectomy","detail":"Alternative with absolute contraindication to thrombolysis or refractory shock after lysis."},{"naam":"Percutaneous catheter-directed therapy","detail":"Catheter-directed local thrombolysis or aspiration technique in submassive PE — less systemic bleeding risk."}]},{"_i":193,"q":"60-year-old man: severe chest pain on arrival.\nRadiation to back/interscapular\nBlood pressure asymmetry 28 mmHg\nECG normal, troponin borderline\nACS or aortic dissection — what supports dissection?","a":["ST elevation with reciprocal changes","Tearing pain + blood pressure asymmetry + normal ECG","Troponin strongly elevated","Radiation to left arm + sweating"],"ex":"Aortic dissection: sudden maximal pain, tearing character, interscapular radiation, blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg, normal ECG and troponin. ACS: pressing pain, radiation to arm/jaw, ST changes, troponin elevated. Blood pressure asymmetry + normal ECG = dissection until proven otherwise.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Aortic dissection vs ACS: two life-threatening causes of acute chest pain. The distinction is crucial — thrombolysis for ACS can be fatal in undiagnosed aortic dissection. Tearing character + maximal pain at onset + blood pressure asymmetry = dissection until proven otherwise.","wiki_redflag":"Thrombolysis in undiagnosed aortic dissection = fatal. Always be aware of the possibility of dissection before starting anticoagulation or lysis. ADD-RS (Aortic Dissection Detection Risk Score) helps quantify clinical suspicion.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Aortic dissection: intimal tear","desc":"Tear in the intima of the aortic wall → blood creates a false lumen → propagation proximally and/or distally → occlusion of branching arteries → ischemia (coronary, renal, mesenteric, spinal)."},{"title":"Blood pressure asymmetry","desc":"Dissection flap occludes the subclavian or axillary artery on one side → arm blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg. Also: neurological deficit (spinal ischemia), renal insufficiency (renal ischemia)."},{"title":"ACS: plaque rupture","desc":"Atherosclerotic plaque tears → thrombus → myocardial ischemia → troponin rises → ST changes. Pain pressing, radiation to arm/jaw/shoulder, improves with nitrates."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Aortic dissection (type A or B)","desc":"Tearing/ripping maximal pain at onset, interscapular radiation, blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg, normal/minimally abnormal ECG and troponin."},{"label":"ACS (STEMI/NSTEMI)","desc":"Pressing, squeezing pain, radiation to arm/jaw, sweating, troponin elevated, ST changes on ECG, symptoms improve with nitrates."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Pleuritic pain, tachycardia, dyspnea, DVT risk factor — D-dimer elevated. Normal troponin unless RV overload."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Type A dissection: emergency surgery (within hours). Type B: blood pressure control (labetalol IV, target SBP <120 mmHg).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"CT angiography aorta","detail":"Gold standard for aortic dissection — with contrast, chest-abdomen-pelvis."},{"naam":"No anticoagulation","detail":"With suspected dissection: no aspirin/heparin until dissection excluded — this can enlarge the false lumen."}]},{"_i":194,"q":"72-year-old woman: acute dyspnea, orthopnea, bilateral basal crackles, ankle edema. Heart failure or COPD exacerbation — which feature is most specific for heart failure?","a":["Prolonged expiration with diffuse wheezing","Elevated JVP + S3 gallop rhythm","Hyperinflation on chest X-ray","Reduced peak flow"],"ex":"Heart failure: elevated JVP (jugular venous distension), S3 gallop rhythm, basal crackles, orthopnea, rapid improvement with diuretics. COPD: prolonged expiration, wheezing, hyperinflation, low peak flow. S3 + elevated JVP is almost pathognomonic for decompensated heart failure.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Heart failure vs COPD: both cause dyspnea but through completely different mechanisms. Venous congestion (elevated JVP, hepatomegaly, edema) and gallop rhythm (S3) are almost pathognomonic for decompensated heart failure. BNP is a powerful biomarker to distinguish them.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Heart failure: backward failure","desc":"Reduced LV output → backward failure → pulmonary congestion → crackles, orthopnea, and with RV failure: elevated JVP, hepatomegaly, peripheral edema."},{"title":"S3 gallop rhythm","desc":"Early-diastolic sound due to sudden deceleration of blood in the rapidly filled, stiff LV wall. Almost pathognomonic for elevated LV filling pressure (HFrEF or HFpEF)."},{"title":"COPD: obstructive mechanism","desc":"Airway obstruction → prolonged expiration, air trapping → hyperinflation (barrel chest, hyperinflation on chest X-ray). No venous congestion (unless cor pulmonale in end-stage COPD)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Decompensated heart failure","desc":"Elevated JVP + S3 gallop rhythm + orthopnea + rapid improvement with furosemide. BNP/NT-proBNP elevated. Echo: reduced EF or elevated filling pressures."},{"label":"COPD exacerbation","desc":"Prolonged expiration + diffuse wheezing + hyperinflation on chest X-ray + reduced peak flow. Elevated CRP. Improves with bronchodilation."},{"label":"Overlap (cor pulmonale in COPD)","desc":"Chronic obstructive lung disease + right heart failure → elevated JVP + edema. BNP moderately elevated. Echo: RV dilation, normal LV function."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Decompensated heart failure: furosemide IV 40-80 mg + oxygen + semi-sitting position.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"BNP/NT-proBNP","detail":"If in doubt: normal BNP (<35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP <125 pg/mL) makes heart failure unlikely — consider COPD as diagnosis."},{"naam":"Echocardiography","detail":"Determines EF, filling pressures, valve disease — essential for diagnosis and treatment guidance."}]}]}
